#* {{quote-book|en|author=James Grissom|title=Follies of God: Tennessee Williams and the Women of the Fog|series=A Borzoi Book|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Alfred A. Knopf|Alfred A[braham] Knopf]]|year=2015|page=50|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6p55BAAAQBAJ&pg=PA50|isbn=978-0-307-26569-2|passage=I &#91;{{w|Tennessee Williams}}&#93; told her &#91;{{w|Eva Le Gallienne}}&#93; I had, indeed, read the plays of Mr. [[w:Henrik Ibsen|[Henrik] Ibsen]], had seen them in numerous productions, and found them to be quite like the act of eating a box of soap flakes, when they were not like two '''months of Sundays''' in church.}}

====Usage notes====
The term is often used negatively, for example, “[[never in a month of Sundays]]” or “not in a month of Sundays”.

